 ## 3. What changed and what did not                                            
                                                                                
 This draft addresses the five scoped items: (1) formatting `CL1`–`CL3` as `CLn 
 · process` in `README.md` and `tools/explain.py` alongside a process-scope     
 disclaimer; (2) hardening `tools/make_record.py` and `tests/red_counts.py` so  
 control `expected`/`outcome` values are derived from score rows, packages are  
 named by role without overwriting cited bytes, and planted test records are    
 version-agnostic (computing the spec version dynamically from `SPEC.md`); (3)  
 replacing `SPEC.md` §5.3's `CL2-3` conditional prose with a single fenced      
 pseudocode block of unchanged meaning; (4) setting the disposition's package   
 reference key to `x-on` in `tools/make_record.py`; and (5) making the `cl1_10` 
 referee in `checker/check.py` total over malformed `checks` and                
 `disagreements` shapes without crashing or accepting booleans/non-integers as  
 indices (with corresponding `SPEC.md` `CL1-10` prose and a fixture per         
 malformed shape in `fixtures/`). We also updated `CHANGELOG.md` and bumped the 
 version string to `0.9.8`. We deliberately left `CL3-3`, signing, the JSON     
 schema, and `record/` untouched.                                               
                                                                                
 ## 4. Where to look hardest                                                    
                                                                                
 The largest surface area and highest risk sit in `tools/make_record.py` (hunks 
 11–16 for `item 2` and `item 4`). Because prior callers and tests pass score   
 rows, control events, and package references in several shapes (inline         
 `scores` arrays/dicts in `INPUTS.json`, external `scores.tsv`/`scores.json`    
 files, embedded `scoreRow` dicts on events, and legacy `on` vs `x-on` fields), 
 `load_scores`, `parse_row`, `role_name`, and `check_packages` accept a wide    
 range of alias keys and file names. A reviewer should look closely at whether  
 that permissiveness in `parse_row` / `load_scores` could silently match an     
 unintended row or mask a malformed score file, and whether `role_name`'s       
 heuristics (`require_role=True` in `resolve` vs `require_role=False` in        
 `check_packages` and `fix_disp`) reject any legitimate package string or let a 
 path-like string slip through when converting `disposition["on"]` to `x-on`    
 (`item 4`). In `tools/explain.py` (`item 1`), also verify whether gating the   
 `CLn · process` formatting of `levelClaimed` in the header on `reached in      
 LEVELS` is what you want when a record claims `CL2` but achieves `FAILED` or   
 `baseline`.                                                                    
 =======                                                                        
 ## 3. What changed and what did not                                            
                                                                                
 The version moved and nothing else did. `SPEC.md`'s header names this release  
 and carries a date later                                                       
 than the header it replaces; the example value in section 4 names the same     
 release; `CHANGELOG.md` gains                                                  
 this release's entry; `README.md`'s status line names this release; this note  
 describes it; and the two                                                      
 example files declare it. Every requirement row in SPEC.md section 5 is the    
 base commit's, id for id and                                                   
 text for text, and section 4's field table is the base commit's cell for cell  
 apart from the example                                                         
 value. Signing, the schema and the kept records are untouched.                 
                                                                                
 ## 4. Where to look hardest                                                    
                                                                                
 Two sentences carry the risk, because each states a fact a reader relies on    
 and each is rewritten here.                                                    
 The first is in `CONTRIBUTING.md`: it explains that an earlier release shipped 
 without a record. It no                                                        
 longer names that release by a version token — the repository's own agreement  
 check refuses a current                                                        
 document that names a version other than this one — so it names the release in 
 words and points at the                                                        
 note that explains it. A reviewer should check that the sentence still says    
 which release it is about                                                      
 and where the note is kept. The second is the cadence sentence in the same     
 document's Releases section,                                                   
 which now carries the Owner's amendment in the Owner's words; a reviewer       
 should check that the amendment                                                
 reads as a sentence in that section, not as a citation of one.                 
